## Coding Conventions

{% if profile.stack.language == "rust" %}
- Prefer `Result<T, E>` and `?` over `unwrap()` in production paths.
- Use `thiserror` for library errors, `anyhow` for application errors.
- Prefer `Arc<Mutex<T>>` over global mutable state.
- Name types in `UpperCamelCase`, functions and variables in `snake_case`.
- Keep functions under 50 lines; cyclomatic complexity under 10.
{% endif %}
{% if profile.stack.language == "python" %}
- Follow PEP 8. Ruff is the enforcer — do not suppress without comment.
- Type-annotate all public functions and methods.
- Use dataclasses or Pydantic models instead of plain dicts for structured data.
- Keep functions under 20 lines (excluding docstrings); cyclomatic complexity under 10.
- Business logic lives in service modules, not in views or serializers.
{% endif %}
{% if profile.stack.language == "dart" %}
- Follow Dart effective style. `dart format` is non-negotiable.
- Use `freezed` for immutable domain models.
- BLoC/Cubit delegates to use-cases; no domain logic in event handlers.
- Keep widget build methods under 30 lines; extract sub-widgets for clarity.
- Prefer `const` constructors wherever possible.
{% endif %}
{% if profile.stack.language == "go" %}
- Follow the official Go style guide. `gofmt` is non-negotiable.
- Return errors explicitly; never ignore `err` return values.
- Use `context.Context` as the first parameter for I/O-bound functions.
- Keep functions under 50 lines; cyclomatic complexity under 15.
- Table-driven tests are the standard for unit tests.
{% endif %}
{% if profile.stack.language == "typescript" %}
- TypeScript strict mode is always on. No `any` without a suppression comment.
- Prefer interfaces over type aliases for object shapes.
- Use destructured options objects when a function takes more than 3 parameters.
- Keep functions under 25 lines; cyclomatic complexity under 10.
- Async/await over raw Promise chains.
{% endif %}
